{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat decisions/D031-split-diagnosis-proxy-scale.md decisions/D039-qwen-frontier-results.md assignments/T-PROF*'","aggregated_output":"# D031 — Why the hybrid split loses on Granite (nsys, T-PROF) and the proxy-scale bias it reveals\nEvidence: artifacts/codexPROF/ANALYSIS.md, cc/nsys_decode/analyze_nsys.py (new profiling CC), artifacts/x4-hs/prof/{split,static}-n12.sqlite (graph-level, same binary, 154 decode tokens after 5 warmup).\nPer token: static n12 5.35 ms (13 graph launches, 110 syncs, GPU busy 1.06 ms = GPU idle ~80%); split n12 7.38 ms (25 graph launches, 194 syncs, GPU busy 2.55 ms). Extra 2.03 ms = 1.45 ms extra GPU graph work (per-slot hot dispatch: 1535 vs 583 kernels in first decode) + 0.37 ms exposed sync/transfers + 0.21 ms host. CUDA graphs ARE reused (no capture loss).\nInterpretation (orchestrator): at batch 1 on this host, decode is dominated by per-layer CPU<->GPU handoff latency, not by expert bytes or GPU compute. The split computes FEWER CPU experts per token (~52 vs 96 at ~73% hot coverage) yet is slower because it pays the handoff in 24 layers instead of 12. T-PROF's best redesign (batched CUDA dispatch with skip IDs, -0.7..-1.1 ms) would only reach ~parity with static.\nTransferability (key factory lesson): Granite experts are 0.84 MiB; the target (~500 GB MoE) has experts ~10-50x larger, so per-layer fixed costs amortize and expert-granular placement (hot GPU / cold CPU, Strata-style) should regain its advantage. Granite-only results systematically favour layer-granular placement. => The factory needs a second proxy that genuinely exceeds VRAM with larger experts before deciding placement granularity; Granite results on placement are labelled \"small-expert regime\".\nMethodology rule: report per-layer fixed cost (handoff latency) alongside per-expert cost; predict crossover expert size = fixed_cost / per_byte_cost before choosing granularity.\n# D039 — Proxy-2 Qwen3-30B-A3B frontier (protocol D038), job job-9f50eae42a 27/27 clean, host quiet (84 monitor samples, no compiler/download)\nImage release-1. Decode t/s median [GPU delta MiB]: K=8 93.5 [15684] | 12 73.3 [14290] | 18 54.6 [12246] | 24 43.6 [10206] | 30 36.6 [8162] | 36 31.7 [6120] | 42 27.7 [4074] | 48 24.4 [1798]. Prefill 160 -> 54 t/s. Elapsed per run (load from page cache + 128 tok): 7.7 -> 11.1 s. K=18 + FA + q8_0 KV: 12066 MiB (-180), 53.9 t/s (-1.3%).\nMemory-fit result: 10x less GPU memory (17.7 GB model -> 1.8 GB resident) for 3.8x slower decode, at unchanged weights (accuracy = Q4_K_M; placement-only).\nModel: 1/decode = t_gpu + K x c; c = (1/24.4 - 1/93.5)/40 = 0.757 ms per CPU layer = 8 experts x 2.72 MiB / 0.757 ms = ~30 GB/s effective CPU expert bandwidth (Granite D022: ~26 GB/s). TRANSFERABLE CONSTANT for this host: CPU expert path is DRAM-bandwidth bound at ~26-30 GB/s for Q4_K.\nPre-registered predictions scored: orchestrator (bytes / 26 GB/s): K=18 50-60 -> 54.6 OK; K=48 20-25 -> 24.4 OK. Planner (extrapolated from Granite per-layer calibration): K=18 8.1 vs 54.6 (-85%), K=30 4.9 vs 36.6, K=41/42 3.6 vs 27.7 -> planner extrapolation FAILED (~7x pessimistic) although correctly labelled \"extrapolated\". Fix: decode model from bytes/bandwidth (host constant) instead of Granite-fitted per-layer time (delegated).\nClassification: runtime-placement trade-off (existing engine features) on proxy-2; the factory's baseline frontier for this model.\n# Assignment T-PROF (campaign c2-resume, x86-main; owner Claude Opus; worker Codex) — profile analysis for D030\nQuestion: why is the hybrid expert split (cc/hybrid_split, decisions/D030) 16-32% SLOWER than static layer offload (--n-cpu-moe) at equal GPU MiB, with the deficit growing as more experts are hot?\nInputs (nsys 2024.6.2 graph-level traces, --sample=none --cpuctxsw=none, -t cuda,nvtx,osrt, --cuda-graph-trace=graph; same binary; chat prompt -n 160 decode): artifacts/x4-hs/prof/split-n12.sqlite and static-n12.sqlite (+ .nsys-rep). Note D008: graph-level traces perturb less; node-level not captured. Read decisions/D008, D011, D019, D030 and cc/hybrid_split/README.md (design: per-slot hot dispatch, router GET_ROWS reorder, CPU cold MUL_MAT_ID, 24 layers each with CPU work).\nDo (Python sqlite3 only, light CPU): per decode token (segment by the per-token pattern, discard the first ~5 tokens as warmup): count of CUDA graph launches, kernel launches outside graphs, cudaStreamSynchronize/cudaMemcpy (by kind/size) calls and time, D2H/H2D bytes, GPU busy time, GPU idle gaps (count + total), and wall time per token; top kernels by total time for each; identify whether split runs ANY kernels inside CUDA graphs (graph reuse broken?). Produce a side-by-side table and attribute the per-token difference (split - static) into buckets: extra GPU kernels, extra syncs/transfers, CPU-side time while GPU idle, graph capture loss.\nThen propose (do not implement) the 2-3 most promising redesigns with a predicted per-token saving each, grounded in the numbers (e.g. CUDA MUL_MAT_ID supporting negative/skip IDs to avoid per-slot dispatch; grouping cold work so only K layers touch the CPU; overlapping CPU cold compute with GPU hot compute via async copies).\nDeliver artifacts/codexPROF/ANALYSIS.md + analyze_nsys.py (reusable tool: a profiling CC for \"per-token decode breakdown from nsys sqlite\") + its pytest on a tiny synthetic sqlite. Allowed writes: cc/nsys_decode/** (the tool), artifacts/codexPROF/**. 40 min, no GPU, no Docker, light CPU. Final message: the table, attribution, proposals, tool usage.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
